\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We fine-tune OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft}, which concatenates DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024dinov2} (1024-d) and SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} (1152-d) patch features into a 2176-dimensional representation per spatial token. We apply LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} (rank 32, $\alpha{=}16$) and augment the action prediction loss with an auxiliary spatial prediction head:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda(t) \cdot \mathcal{L}_\text{aux}(h(\phi_v(o_t)), s_t^*),
\quad \lambda(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t / 50000)
\label{eq:total_loss}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda(t)$ decays exponentially so that spatial gradients shape early representations without dominating late-stage action refinement. The auxiliary head is discarded at inference.

\textbf{BEV occupancy head.} The head (1.2M parameters) maps the first 256 patch tokens to a $64{\times}64$ binary occupancy grid: a linear projection ($2176{\to}256$) reshaped to $16{\times}16$, two transposed convolutions ($256{\to}128{\to}64$, kernel 4, stride 2) with BatchNorm and GELU, and a $1{\times}1$ convolution with sigmoid. Ground-truth grids are rendered from MuJoCo state replay. Training uses focal loss~\cite{lin2017focal} ($\alpha{=}0.25$, $\gamma{=}2.0$).

\textbf{Monocular depth head.} Same architecture with ReLU output and smooth-L1 loss against egocentric depth maps rendered at $64{\times}64$ from MuJoCo. This head provides an egocentric spatial reference frame, complementing the allocentric BEV occupancy.

\subsection{Ablation Design}
\label{sec:ablation}

We evaluate twelve conditions that systematically vary gradient flow, label content, normalization type, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Vanilla}: action loss only (baseline).
\item \textbf{Full OCC}: BEV occupancy with ground-truth labels and BatchNorm; gradients flow through both encoders.
\item \textbf{Full OCC+LN}: same as Full OCC but with LayerNorm replacing BatchNorm2d; tests whether gradient flow benefits are normalization-dependent.
\item \textbf{Random OCC}: random binary labels with matched gradient flow; isolates gradient regularization from spatial content.
\item \textbf{Detach OCC}: ground-truth labels with encoder gradients blocked (\texttt{stop\_gradient}) and BatchNorm; tests whether gradient flow is necessary.
\item \textbf{Detach+LN}: same as Detach OCC but with LayerNorm replacing BatchNorm2d; isolates the contribution of normalization design from gradient flow absence.
\item \textbf{Depth}: egocentric depth maps with gradient flow; compares allocentric vs.\ egocentric spatial frames.
\item \textbf{Cutoff@\{15K,25K,35K,45K\}}: occupancy supervision for the first 15K--45K of 50K steps. Setting the loss weight to zero retains the auxiliary head forward pass; the post-cutoff period operates in Detach mode (BN interference without gradient compensation).
\item \textbf{Reverse Cutoff}: occupancy supervision for only the last 15K steps; tests whether late-stage gradient flow can rescue early Detach-mode training.
\end{itemize}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design} summarizes what each pairwise comparison isolates.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation design: each comparison isolates one factor.}
\label{tab:ablation_design}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Comparison & Isolated Factor \\
\midrule
Full OCC vs.\ Vanilla & Auxiliary spatial supervision \\
Random vs.\ Full OCC & Spatial content vs.\ gradient flow \\
Detach vs.\ Vanilla & Necessity of encoder gradient flow \\
Detach+LN vs.\ Detach OCC & Normalization (BN vs.\ LN) without gradient flow \\
Full OCC+LN vs.\ Full OCC & Normalization (BN vs.\ LN) with gradient flow \\
$2{\times}2$ factorial & Normalization $\times$ gradient flow interaction \\
Depth vs.\ Full OCC & Egocentric vs.\ allocentric frame \\
Cutoff@\{15K--45K\} vs.\ Full OCC & Temporal extent of gradient flow \\
Reverse Cutoff vs.\ Cutoff@35K & Early vs.\ late gradient flow \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation}
\label{sec:evaluation}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, which places obstacles in close proximity to targets across 10 tabletop manipulation tasks drawn from LIBERO-Spatial~\cite{liu2023libero}. We report \textbf{Task Success Rate (TSR)} and \textbf{Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR)}, where a collision is registered when the L1 displacement of the obstacle's \texttt{body\_xpos} exceeds $10^{-3}$. Each condition uses 3+ seeds (Depth uses 4) with 50 rollouts per task per seed. Bootstrap 95\% CIs are computed via 10{,}000 hierarchical resamples. Training uses a batch size of 8, constant learning rate $5 \times 10^{-4}$, and runs for 50K steps on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (98\,GB); each run takes $\sim$12.9 hours. Full training details are in Appendix~\ref{app:training}.

\section{Introduction}

Vision-language-action (VLA) models map visual observations and language instructions directly to robot actions, achieving high task success on manipulation benchmarks~\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla, kim2025oft}. Yet task success does not imply behavioral safety. On SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a fine-tuned OpenVLA-OFT completes 90.7\% of tasks but avoids collisions in only 78.0\% of episodes; on the three most constrained tasks, collision rates reach 53--93\%.

Auxiliary spatial prediction (occupancy grids, depth maps, point clouds) improves VLA task success by injecting 3D structure into vision encoders~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, yuan2025depthvla, wu2025spatialvla}. Separately, safety methods apply runtime action filtering~\cite{hu2025vlsa} or constrained RL~\cite{zhang2025safevla} to reduce collisions. These two threads remain disconnected: no spatial method evaluates collision avoidance, and no safety method leverages spatial representations. Whether auxiliary spatial supervision affects safety, and through what mechanism, is unknown.

We present a systematic factorial study isolating how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance. Twelve conditions on OpenVLA-OFT~\cite{kim2024openvla, kim2025oft} with LoRA vary gradient flow, label content, normalization type, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent. Figure~\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the setup and key finding. Our contributions:

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Overview.} \textbf{(a)}~VLA policies deployed in contact-rich environments lack collision awareness; a fine-tuned OpenVLA-OFT achieves 90.7\% task success but only 78.0\% collision avoidance on SafeLIBERO. \textbf{(b)}~We attach an auxiliary occupancy prediction head to the vision encoders; it provides gradient flow during training and is discarded at inference (zero cost). The normalization layer (BN/LN) at the encoder output is a critical design choice. \textbf{(c)}~A $2{\times}2$ factorial reveals a crossover interaction ($p{=}0.025$): BatchNorm benefits from auxiliary gradient flow (+5.1\,pp), while LayerNorm is harmed ($-$2.2\,pp). These are opposite effects of the same mechanism.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Crossover interaction.} Normalization type and gradient flow interact rather than compose additively (interaction effect: 7.3\,pp, $p{=}0.025$). BatchNorm with gradient flow maintains CAR comparable to Vanilla (0.797 vs.\ 0.780), but without gradient flow causes catastrophic representational drift (CKA $\downarrow$0.31--0.54). LayerNorm eliminates drift under detachment (CKA $\geq$0.96) but consistently degrades safety when gradient flow is present (Full OCC+LN 0.743, $-$3.7\,pp), the worst configuration tested. This constitutes a representation-behavior dissociation: the configuration that best preserves pretrained representations produces the worst safety. Gradient flow benefits are BatchNorm-mediated, not an intrinsic property of auxiliary gradients. Label content does not affect outcomes.

    \item \textbf{Factorial decomposition reveals hidden interactions.} Prior VLA studies vary auxiliary tasks one factor at a time, which cannot detect interactions. Our $2{\times}2$ factorial design (Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design}) reveals a crossover interaction that single-factor ablations would miss: gradient flow helps under BN but hurts under LN. This demonstrates that factorial decomposition is necessary for reliable conclusions about auxiliary task effects in VLA training.

    \item \textbf{Zero-cost guideline.} Maintain BatchNorm with gradient flow, the only configuration that preserves CAR. If gradient flow is unavailable, substitute LayerNorm to prevent representational drift. Never combine LayerNorm with gradient flow. No ground-truth spatial labels are required; the auxiliary head is discarded after training at zero inference cost.
\end{enumerate}

